Given perfect play they probably shouldn't, and I've only occasionally had 
issues with chests. Once I had a dragon that kept picking my chest up and 
following me with it, I think I eventually tracked it down about 5 dungeon 
levels lower than it started. More recently I had a game where a gelatinous 
cube ate the chest then jumped down a trapdoor. Ok, so I got everything 
(except the chest) back again eventually, but my point is simply that 
writing down notes on paper takes the notes outside the scope where RNG or 
player stupidity can combine to lose the information. Roger Broadbent 
maintains that he loses his paper notes more often than his caches go 
astray, so in his case it sounds fair enough, but for those of us with 
regular YASDs we probably have more than enough potential to mess up any 
scheme for recording information in-game.

